<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Pack for a Trip. – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Pack for a Trip.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Facilitate a simple discussion among classmates. concept: Link historical events to current events for class discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion about beach trips by asking simple questions, providing sentence starters, and using gestures (e.g., saying, 'What do you bring to the beach?' while pointing to sunscreen or showing a packing checklist). Can link historical beach-related events to current activities with guided comparisons (e.g., saying, 'Look at the pictures of people at the beach. Which one is from the past and which one is from today?' while students identify clothing and activities from each period). Can encourage classmates to compare beach activities from the past and present using structured prompts and gestures (e.g., 'How is it different now?' while pointing to an old-fashioned bathing suit).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays two large pictures: one showing a beach scene from the past (e.g., early 1900s, with long swimsuits and no sunscreen), and another showing a modern beach scene (e.g., today, with small swimsuits, sunscreen, beach ball, cooler). The teacher points to items and asks, "What do you see?" (e.g., 'swimsuit', 'sun', 'sand'). "What do people do?" (e.g., 'swim', 'relax'). The teacher introduces the concept of "past" and "today."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models facilitating a simple discussion by pointing to the pictures and asking comparison questions. "Look at the pictures. What do people wear in the past? What do they wear today? Is it different?" The teacher models simple sentence starters for comparison: "In the past, people wore...", "Today, we wear...". The teacher also models polite questions like, "What do you think?" or "Can you tell me more?" using gestures to encourage participation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'past' and 'today' and the concept of comparing.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'past' talking about yesterday or a long, long time ago?" (Expected answer: A long time ago)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we looking for things that are the same or different in the pictures?" (Expected answer: Different)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents two clear, contrasting pictures of people at the beach: one from the past (e.g., 1920s) and one from the present day, clearly showing different clothing, activities, and items. Accompanying each picture are short, simple phrases describing what people *did* or *do* and what they *packed* or *pack*. (e.g., Past picture: "People wore big swimsuits. They built sandcastles. They didn't use sunscreen." Present picture: "People wear small swimsuits. They swim and play with a beach ball. They always pack sunscreen.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two large, labeled picture cards (Past Beach Scene, Present Beach Scene). Simple written descriptions below each picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Linking historical events to current events for class discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students respond to gist questions to identify key differences and similarities.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which picture shows people from a long time ago?" (Students point)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do people bring to the beach today? Point to it." (e.g., 'sunscreen', 'cooler', 'beach ball')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did people have flip-flops in the past?" (No)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Facilitating discussion using simple questions and sentence starters. Linking past/present events using present simple (affirmative and question forms: 'Do you like to swim?'), imperatives ('Please, bring your swimsuit.'), and 'going to' ('I am going to the beach this weekend.'). Vocabulary: beach, towel, sunscreen, swimsuit, cooler, flip-flops, sun hat, swim, relax, pack. Pronunciation: /b/ in 'beach', /p/ in 'pack'. Pragmatic: Describing trips and routines. Sociolinguistic: Giving instructions politely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Comparison Sentences (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters on the board: "In the past, people often ____." "Today, we always ____." Students work individually or in pairs to complete the sentences orally, using vocabulary from the beach pictures (e.g., "In the past, people often wore big swimsuits. Today, we always wear small swimsuits and bring sunscreen."). The teacher pays attention to pronunciation of /b/ and /p/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Polite Packing Mediator (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in groups of three. Student A acts as the "trip planner" and gives polite packing instructions using imperatives (e.g., "Please pack your towel." "Don't forget your flip-flops."). Student B acts as the "mediator." Student B rephrases Student A's instructions as a question to Student C, linking to the past/present comparison (e.g., "Maria says 'Please pack your towel.' What about sunscreen? Did people use sunscreen in the past?"). Student C responds, connecting to the current packing checklist. The teacher encourages polite language and simple comparisons.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "How Is It Different?" Discussion (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Using the past and present beach pictures, they take turns facilitating a mini-discussion. One student asks questions like, "What do people do today? How is it different from the past?" or "What do you like to do at the beach? Did people do that before?" The other student answers and asks another question to keep the discussion going. They use gestures to point to elements in the pictures. The teacher circulates, providing sentence starters like, "What do you think?" or "Can you tell me more about..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "Our Beach Trip: Past and Present Packing Discussion"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4). Each group uses their previously created "Packing Checklist" (Project 1). The task is for each group to present their checklist and facilitate a simple discussion with another group (or the class) about what they are *going to* pack for a beach trip today, and how it *compares* to what people packed in the past, using the provided pictures as a reference. One student in each group acts as the designated "facilitator" for this discussion (mediation role).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation and Facilitated Discussion.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The facilitator introduces their group's packing checklist: "This is our checklist. We are going to pack a swimsuit, sunscreen, and a towel."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The facilitator then encourages discussion and comparison with the audience/other group using simple questions and gestures: "What do you bring to the beach? Do you always pack a cooler? Did people bring coolers in the past? How is it different now?" (Pointing to the past/present pictures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group members support the facilitator by pointing to items on the checklist or pictures, using simple sentences like, "I always pack my sun hat!" or "In the past, they didn't have floaties."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Emphasis is on using the target vocabulary, grammar (present simple, imperatives, 'going to'), and facilitating a conversation with simple questions and comparative phrases.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on the facilitator's ability to engage classmates, link past/present, and use target language. Peer feedback on the clarity of the discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, particularly the designated facilitator, will be assessed during the "Our Beach Trip: Past and Present Packing Discussion" (Stage 4). The teacher observes their ability to facilitate, ask questions, use sentence starters for comparison, and engage their peers effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can facilitate a simple discussion about beach trips by asking simple questions (e.g., "What do you bring?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can link activities from the past and present with guided comparisons (e.g., "How is it different now?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses appropriate target vocabulary (e.g., 'sunscreen', 'swimsuit', 'cooler', 'beach ball', 'flip-flops').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple present tense, imperatives, and 'going to' correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of words with /b/ (beach, bucket) and /p/ (pack, picnic) is intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively encourages classmates to participate and responds to their input.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did I ask questions to help my friends talk about packing for the beach? (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did I help my friends compare beach trips from the past and today? (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy about helping my friends talk about the beach and what to pack?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult about comparing beach trips from the past and today?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The mediation skills were challenging but students managed with guided prompts. The visual comparison of past/present beach scenes was effective for linking historical events. Some students needed more explicit modeling of comparative phrases ("In the past...", "Today..."). Using gestures to prompt comparison was very helpful. I should provide more structured sentence frames for the "facilitator" role in future mediation tasks. The focus on /b/ and /p/ sounds was well integrated. The polite imperatives were also used adequately.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Let's Pack for a Trip" theme. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and a new scenario, building on students' ability to discuss routines and plans ('going to'), and continuing to develop their communicative competences in a new context, possibly involving family or friends' activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>